<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  PipMon.ppj : Papyrus project for the PIP-MON API surface.

  SCHEMA, and the mistake worth recording
    The first version of this file used the SKYRIM project shape
    (PapyrusCompile / CompileDisplayName / Files). Fallout 4 uses a different
    one, and the XSD shipped beside PapyrusCompiler.exe is authoritative:

        PapyrusProject  (root, with Output/Flags/Asm/Optimize attributes)
          Imports / Import   (one per import directory, semicolon-free)
          Folders / Folder
          Scripts / Script   (one per .psc to compile)

    Getting this wrong does not error usefully. PapyrusCompiler accepted the
    Skyrim-shaped file, reported nothing, and silently produced no output:
    a green-looking invocation that compiled nothing. When a Papyrus build
    emits nothing, check the project schema against the shipped XSD before
    suspecting the compiler.

  WHY A .ppj AT ALL
    Two reasons, one mechanical and one structural.

    Mechanical: PapyrusCompiler parses its own command line with a splitter
    that does not tolerate the spaces in this repo's path, and the base-script
    import directory lives under "Program Files (x86)". A project file names
    sources by relative path from its own directory, so no spaced absolute
    path is ever handed to the argument parser.

    Structural: section 23.3 records that FO4 namespaced identifiers must be
    parents of each other in the file system, and that namespaced scripts are
    compiled through a project file. Compiling the surface as a project is
    the supported path for anything with a namespace, not a workaround.
-->
<PapyrusProject Output="build" Flags="papyrus_flags.flg">
  <Imports>
    <Import>base</Import>
  </Imports>
  <Scripts>
    <Script>PipMon.psc</Script>
  </Scripts>
</PapyrusProject>
